  "generated_text": "1. A number is a multiple of 6 if it is a multiple of both 2 and 3.\n2. A number is a multiple of 2 if its last digit is even.\n3. A number is a multiple of 3 if the sum of its digits is a multiple of 3.\n\nGiven the number \\(52\\,28\\square\\):\n- The last digit must be even. Therefore, the last digit can be 0 or 8.\n- The sum of the digits of \\(52\\,28\\square\\) must be a multiple of 3.\n\nLet's calculate the sum of the known digits:\n\\[ 5 + 2 + 2 + 8 = 17 \\]\n\nIf the last digit is 0:\n\\[ 17 + 0 = 17 \\]\nwhich is already a multiple of 3.\n\nIf the last digit is 8:\n\\[ 17 + 8 = 25 \\]\nwhich is not a multiple of 3.\n\nTherefore, the last digit must be 0.\n\nThe digit represented by \\(\\square\\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[0, '0']",
    "gold_answer": "Since $52\\,28\\square$ is a multiple of 6, then it must be a multiple of 2 and a multiple of 3.\n\nSince it is a multiple of 2, the digit represented by $\\square$ must be even. Since it is a multiple of 3, the sum of its digits is divisible by 3.\n\nThe sum of its digits is $5+2+2+8+\\square = 17+\\square$.\n\nSince $\\square$ is even, the possible sums of digits are 17, 19, 21, 23, 25 (for the possible values 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 for $\\square$).\n\nOf these possibilities, only 21 is divisible by 3, so $\\square$ must equal $\\boxed{4}$.\n\nWe can check that $52\\,284$ is divisible by 6.\n\n(An alternate approach would have been to use a calculator and test each of the  five possible values for $\\square$ by dividing the resulting values of $52\\,28\\square$ by 6.)",
    "is_correct": false
  },
